    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d36">

                <pb facs="0052" n="36" sameAs="frus:frus1947v08/pg36"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710 Consultation 4/8–1847: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chairman of the United States Delegation</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Marshall</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Petrópolis</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-08-18T01:00:00-03:00">August 18, 1947—1
                            a.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>20. Conference summary 2. First plenary session August 16 approved decisions
                    Preliminary Meeting report Summary 1 plus resolutions appreciation to Brazil,
                    homage to memory Roosevelt, Berreta (Uruguay)<anchor n="68" xml:id="d36-f1"/>
                    and welcome to Lie.<anchor n="69" xml:id="d36-f2"/> Speeches by Bramuglia
                    (Argentina) Vergara (Chile) Marques Castro (Uruguay) and Cáceres (Honduras).
                    Vergara stressed need economic cooperation. Marques Castro emphasized necessity
                    social and economic development as basis democracy and peace, in effect
                    restating Rodríguez Larreta<anchor n="70" xml:id="d36-f3"/> doctrine but making
                    clear that before community can act against violation human rights, must have
                    juridical foundation based on prior agreement. Although reference unclear, he
                    suggested distinction in treaty between procedures applicable to American attack
                    and those to non-American attack. He stressed deep concern re armaments race and
                    emphasized need give “juridical” basis to Rio agreement. In subsequent
                    conversations Uruguayan delegates indicated this means Uruguay will seek as
                    irreducible minimum adoption resolution favoring unlimited arbitration or
                    judicial settlement on ground Rio treaty would be meaningless without this
                    foundation.</p>
                <p>Bramuglia repeated Perón<anchor n="71" xml:id="d36-f4"/> thesis re dangers
                    capitalism and imperialism of either right or left. He mentioned economic
                    cooperation only in passing and made no proposals.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d36-f1" n="68" xml:id="d36-f1-content">
                <p>Tomás Berreta, the President of Uruguay, whose death occurred on August 2,
                    1947.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d36-f2" n="69" xml:id="d36-f2-content">
                <p>Trygve Lie, Secretary General of the United Nations.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d36-f3" n="70" xml:id="d36-f3-content">
                <p>Former Minister for Foreign Affairs in Uruguay.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d36-f4" n="71" xml:id="d36-f4-content">
                <p>Juan Perón, President of Argentina.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
